  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
